---
import Layout from "../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Breadcrumbs from "../../../components/layout/Breadcrumbs.astro";
import { COMPANY } from "../../../data/company";
---

<Layout
  title="Investment Mortgages: A Complete Guide | Cliffco Mortgage Bankers"
  description="How investment property loans work, what they require, and which type is right for you. From single-family rentals to small multi-family buildings."
  type="article"
>
  <Breadcrumbs trail={[
    { name: "Home", href: "/" },
    { name: "Learn", href: "/learn/" },
    { name: "Investment Mortgage Guide", href: "/learn/what-is-an-investment-mortgage/" },
  ]} />

  <section class="container-wide pt-12 pb-12">
    <p class="eyebrow">Investment</p>
    <h1 class="mt-3" style="font-size: var(--text-display-lg);">Investment Mortgages: A Complete Guide</h1>
    <p class="lead mt-6">
      Investment property financing works differently from buying a home you'll live in. The requirements
      are stricter, the rates are higher, and the loan types are more varied. Here's what you need to know.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="bg-white pb-12">
    <div class="container-prose">

      <h2>What Is an Investment Mortgage?</h2>
      <p>
        An investment mortgage finances a property you intend to rent out or hold for appreciation rather
        than live in yourself. Because the borrower doesn't occupy the property, lenders view investment
        loans as higher risk than primary residence loans. That difference shows up in the rates, down
        payment requirements, and qualification standards.
      </p>

      <h2>How Investment Mortgages Differ From Regular Home Loans</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Rates:</strong> Expect rates that run roughly 0.5% to 0.75% higher than a comparable primary residence loan.</li>
        <li><strong>Credit score:</strong> Most programs require a 680 minimum. To access the best pricing, 740 or above helps significantly.</li>
        <li><strong>Down payment:</strong> Usually 15% to 25%, depending on loan type and property. Primary home programs with 3% to 5% down are not available for investment properties.</li>
        <li><strong>Income verification:</strong> Stricter documentation requirements apply, whether you're a W-2 employee or self-employed.</li>
        <li><strong>Reserves:</strong> Lenders typically want to see 6 to 12 months of payments in reserve after closing, not including the down payment.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Types of Investment Property Loans</h2>
      <h3>Conventional investment loans</h3>
      <p>
        Follow Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac guidelines. Available for single-family rentals and 2-to-4 unit
        properties. Require standard income documentation (W-2s, tax returns) and carry the requirements
        listed above. These are the baseline option for W-2 borrowers with strong credit.
      </p>

      <h3>Non-QM investment loans</h3>
      <p>
        For borrowers who don't fit conventional guidelines, non-QM programs allow alternative income
        documentation. Bank statements, 1099 income, and P&amp;L statements can all be used. Non-QM
        investment loans are commonly used by self-employed investors who show lower taxable income after
        business deductions.
      </p>

      <h3>DSCR loans</h3>
      <p>
        The property's rental income does the qualifying work, not the borrower's personal income. No
        tax returns, no W-2s. Just the property's cash flow relative to the debt payment. DSCR loans
        are covered in depth in our <a href="/learn/dscr-loans-real-estate-investors-guide/">DSCR loan guide</a>.
      </p>

      <h3>FHA multi-family (owner-occupied)</h3>
      <p>
        FHA loans are technically primary residence products, but they can function as investment tools
        through what's called house hacking. If you buy a duplex, triplex, or fourplex and live in one
        unit for at least one year, you can use FHA financing with as little as 3.5% down. The rental
        income from the other units can help offset your mortgage payment. After one year of owner
        occupancy, some borrowers move out and keep the property as a pure rental.
      </p>

      <h2>What Property Types Qualify?</h2>
      <p>Most investment mortgage programs cover:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Single-family rentals</li>
        <li>Duplexes, triplexes, and fourplexes (2-4 units)</li>
        <li>Condominiums (warrantable and, through non-QM, non-warrantable)</li>
        <li>Short-term rental properties (through DSCR programs that accept STR income)</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>How Rental Income Factors Into Qualification</h2>
      <p>
        When using a conventional investment loan, lenders typically count 75% of the subject property's
        rental income toward your qualifying income. That 25% haircut accounts for vacancy, management
        costs, and maintenance. With a DSCR loan, the math works differently: the full rent is measured
        against the full debt payment as a ratio, with no personal income entering the picture.
      </p>

      <h2>Steps to Qualify for an Investment Mortgage</h2>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Strengthen your credit.</strong> Get your score above 700 if possible. Pay down revolving balances and avoid opening new accounts before applying.</li>
        <li><strong>Build your reserves.</strong> Lenders want to see that you can cover 6 to 12 months of payments even if the property sits vacant for a period.</li>
        <li><strong>Gather your documentation.</strong> W-2s and tax returns for conventional loans; bank statements or rental operating statements for non-QM or DSCR.</li>
        <li><strong>Research the local market.</strong> Understand what comparable properties rent for. That data matters both to the appraisal and to your DSCR calculation.</li>
        <li><strong>Have a management plan.</strong> Lenders sometimes ask whether you'll self-manage or use a property manager, especially for out-of-state purchases.</li>
        <li><strong>Work with a lender who does this regularly.</strong> Investment loans have more moving parts than primary home loans. An experienced team makes the process faster and avoids costly surprises.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>Can I Put Less Than 20% Down?</h2>
      <p>
        In most cases, 20% to 25% down is the standard for investment properties. A few paths to a
        lower down payment exist. FHA financing on owner-occupied multi-family (house hacking) allows as
        little as 3.5% down. Some non-QM programs will consider 10% down for well-qualified borrowers
        with strong credit and reserves. These are exceptions rather than the rule, and they typically
        come with higher rates.
      </p>

      <h2>How to Choose the Right Loan Type</h2>
      <p>
        The best loan for your situation depends on a handful of factors: your credit profile, how you
        earn your income, how many properties you already own, whether the property is a long-term rental
        or a short-term rental, and how long you plan to hold it. A Cliffco loan officer can map your
        situation to the right product quickly. We originate conventional, non-QM, and DSCR investment
        loans across 32 states.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="accent-gradient">
    <div class="container-wide py-16 text-center">
      <h2 class="text-white" style="font-size: var(--text-h2);">Ready to talk through your options?</h2>
      <p class="lead mt-4 text-white/85">A licensed Cliffco loan officer responds in one business day.</p>
      <div class="mt-8 flex justify-center gap-3 flex-wrap">
        <a href="/get-started/" class="btn btn-primary !bg-white !text-[var(--color-rich-black)]">Get a pre-qual</a>
        <a href={`tel:${COMPANY.hq.phoneE164}`} class="btn btn-secondary !border-white !text-white">{COMPANY.hq.phone}</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
